  abstract={Mitigating self-interference (SI) is vital for advancing simultaneous transmit and receive (STAR) systems. The noise and linearity of the canceller impose limitations on SI power handling. This article proposes a novel dual-path SI cancelling scheme for high-power SI handling. The proposed approach breaks self-interference cancellation (SIC) tradeoffs between operating frequency, SI power handling, and canceller noise/distortion by using a frequency-translated (FT) SI canceller and an auxiliary FT noise-cancelling (NC) path that cancels the noise/distortion from the primary canceller. This approach can work well for both full-duplex (FD) and frequency-division duplex (FDD) schemes. The 65-nm CMOS implementation achieves ~45-dB SIC for 12-dBm receiver (RX) SI peak power (9-dBm avg.) with only 4-dB noise figure (NF) degradation and 207-mW power consumption at 600 MHz. The SIC approach enables a blocker-1 dB with respect to SI of > 16 dBm ( $8\times $  higher than prior work) while operating from 0.5 to 1.3 GHz. Therefore, this implementation can support watt-level SI power handling with transmitter (TX)–RX isolation > 15 dB. This is demonstrated through measurements that show 30-dBm TX power handling with 22-dB initial isolation from the circulator.},
  keywords={Interference cancellation;Distortion;Degradation;Linearity;Full-duplex system;Noise cancellation;Distortion-cancelling;dual-path;frequency-division duplex (FDD);frequency-translated noise-cancelling self-interference cancellation (FT-NCSIC);frequency-translation;full-duplex (FD);NC-self-interference cancellation (SIC);noise-cancelling (NC);radar;self-interference (SI);SI power handling;simultaneous transmit and receive (STAR);third-order intermodulation product;transmitter (TX) leakage},
