  volume={59},
  number={7},
  pages={2297-2309},
  abstract={This article presents a novel static random access memory computing-in-memory (SRAM-CIM) structure designed for high-precision multiply-and-accumulate (MAC) operations with high energy efficiency (EF), high readout accuracy, and short compute latency. The proposed device employs 1) a time-domain incremental-accumulation (TDIA) scheme to enable high-accumulation MAC operations while maintaining a large signal margin across MAC values (MACVs), 2) a dynamic differential-reference (D2REF) scheme based on software-hardware co-design to reduce read energy consumption, and 3) a low-dMACV-aware recursive time-to-digital converter (LMAR-TDC) for implementation with the D2REF scheme to further suppress readout energy consumption. A 28 nm 1 Mb SRAM-CIM macro fabricated using foundry-provided compact 6T-SRAM cells achieved EF of 39.31 TOPS/W and compute latency of 6.6 ns for 8b-MAC operations with 64 accumulations per cycle and near-full output precision (22b).},
  keywords={SRAM cells;Time-domain analysis;Energy consumption;Artificial intelligence;In-memory computing;Inference mechanisms;Edge AI;Artificial intelligence (AI);computing-in-memory (CIM);inference;multiply-and-accumulate (MAC);static random access memory (SRAM)},
